-- title:
--   Proof of Claim 1, p. A1 (PDF 32) — formula (1) is the conditional expectation of the true value given the measurement
-- statement:
--   Let $f \ge 0$ be a measurable joint density of a pair $(P, m)$ on $\mathbb R^2$, so that the law $\mu$ of $(P, m)$ is a probability measure with density $f$, and assume $E|P| < \infty$. Define
--   $$Q(m) = \frac{\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} P\, f(P,m)\, dP}{\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(P,m)\, dP}.$$
--   Then $Q(m)$ is a version of the conditional expectation of the true value given the measurement:
--   $$Q(m) = E[\,P \mid m\,] \quad \text{almost surely.}$$
--
--   This is the fact the proof of Claim 1 uses: on stopping and choosing prototype $i$ after observing $m_i$, the expected value obtained is $Q_i(m_i)$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The conditional expectation is taken with respect to the σ-algebra generated by the second coordinate. The paper prints $dm$ in the denominator of (1); it is read as $dP$, without which the formula is not a conditional expectation.
-- source:
--   Bhaskaran and Erat, Optimal Prototyping with Noisy Measurements, author manuscript of 6 Nov 2024 (MSOM 2025, doi:10.1287/msom.2024.1133), p. A1 (PDF 32), proof of Claim 1, first sentence; (1), p. 10; (A.1), p. A1

import Mathlib
